    "q-334865": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334865",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.20$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.20)≈0.234\\,375.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.08,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.08)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.08,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.08)≈\\frac{0.234\\,375}{39.0625}=0.006.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
